{"id":"e3f00f81-8e96-4051-9ded-2dcbb6345385","object":"chat.completion","created":1784985461,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Validate input\\n    if 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required fields: 'nodes' and 'required_symbols'\\\")\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    \\n    # Build id -> node map, children map, and ancestor sets\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        children[nid] = []\\n    # Build parent-child relationships\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            if parent in children:\\n                children[parent].append(node['id'])\\n    # Build ancestors for each node (excluding self)\\n    ancestors = {}\\n    def compute_ancestors(nid, current_ancestors):\\n        # Use parent chain\\n        anc = set()\\n        p = node_map[nid].get('parent')\\n        while p is not None:\\n            anc.add(p)\\n            p = node_map[p].get('parent')\\n        ancestors[nid] = anc\\n    for nid in node_map:\\n        compute_ancestors(nid, set())\\n    \\n    # Initialize data structures\\n    retained_ids = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed_ids = []\\n    symbol_count = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        symbol_count[sym] = symbol_count.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    \\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    \\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if sym in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        # Check for retained descendants\\n        has_retained_descendant = False\\n        for other_id in list(retained_ids):\\n            if other_id == nid:\\n                continue\\n            if nid in ancestors[other_id]:\\n                has_retained_descendant = True\\n                break\\n        if has_retained_descendant:\\n            continue\\n        # Check required symbols after removal\\n        removal_ok = True\\n        for req_sym in required_symbols:\\n            if symbol_count[req_sym] == 1 and sym == req_sym:\\n                removal_ok = False\\n                break\\n        if not removal_ok:\\n            continue\\n        # Perform removal\\n        retained_ids.remove(nid)\\n        removed_ids.append(nid)\\n        symbol_count[sym] -= 1\\n    \\n    retained_ids_sorted = sorted(retained_ids)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_ids,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids_sorted\\n    }\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function solve(case) that implements grammar-guided node reduction based on the description. The case is a payload object with nodes (list of objects with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols (list of strings). The task is to determine which nodes to remove and retain, following rules: never remove root, required symbol, or node with retained children; tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented; use node ID as stable tie break at equal depth; return removed_node_ids (ordered by removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted). Also: deeper nodes before shallower ones (i.e., process deeper first). The root is the node with parent null. The required symbols are those that must remain in the tree after removal. The utility might be for something? Not used per description? The description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So algorithm: traverse nodes sorted by depth descending, then node id ascending (for tie). For each node that is removable (removable==True) and not required (its symbol not in required_symbols?), but also must ensure that its removal does not cause any required symbol to be absent. But careful: required_symbols are symbols that must be represented in the tree. The root is always retained (non-removable). Also, a node cannot be removed if it has retained children (i.e., any descendant that is retained). So we need to simulate removal in order. A common approach: start with all nodes as potentially retained. Then iterate nodes in the order (depth desc, id asc). For each node that is removable and its symbol not required (or more precisely, we must check that after removal, all required symbols still appear somewhere in the tree among retained nodes). Also, we must not remove a node if any of its children (recursive) are retained. Since we process deeper first, when we consider a node, its children have already been considered (deeper). So if any child is retained, we cannot remove the parent. So the decision for a node depends on whether any descendant is retained. That is consistent: process depth descending, mark nodes as removed or retained. For each node, we check: if it's removable and not a required symbol node (but maybe a node with required symbol could be removable? The spec says \"never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So a node with symbol that is in required_symbols cannot be removed. Also the root is always non-removable anyway. So we should also ensure we don't remove a node whose symbol is required. But the required_symbols list may contain symbols of nodes that are multiple levels. So we need to check the symbol of this node. Also, even if its symbol is not required, we must ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still present. That means we must check that for each required symbol, there exists at least one retained node with that symbol after removal (considering that this node may be the only one providing that symbol). However, since we are processing node by node, when considering removal, we haven't removed it yet. We tentatively remove it and see if all required symbols still exist among the currently retained set (including nodes not yet processed? Actually nodes deeper have already been decided, nodes shallower not yet decided but they are still retained because we haven't considered removing them yet. So we must check that there is at least one node with the required symbol among the set of nodes that are currently retained (including those not yet processed because they will be considered later but are retained now). That is safe because if we later remove that node, we would have prevented removal if it would cause loss. So algorithm: have a set retained_ids and removed_ids. Initially all nodes are retained (set retained_ids = all ids). Process nodes in order depth desc, id asc. For each node:\n- If not removable or its symbol in required_symbols, skip (cannot remove).\n- Else, tentative remove: check if for every required symbol, there exists at least one node in retained_ids (excluding this node) that has that symbol. Also check that no children (or descendants) are currently retained? But because we process deeper first, any descendant that is retained would have been processed and if removed, it would have been removed already. So if there is a retained descendant, it would still be in retained_ids. So we can check if any retained node has this node's id as an ancestor? Simpler: check if any retained node's parent chain includes this node. We can build a parent map. Then for the candidate node, we check if any retained node is a descendant (including direct children) by seeing if there exists a retained node that has this node as an ancestor. Since we have parent map, we can do BFS/DFS from node and see if any descendant is in retained_ids. That is O(n) per node, but n is small. Alternatively, since we process depth descending, we can maintain a set of \"blocked\" nodes where removal is prevented because they have retained children. But we could also compute by checking all retained descendants. The spec says \"never remove a node with retained children.\" It doesn't say descendants, but logically if any descendant is retained, the node has at least one retained child (the path). However \"retained children\" might mean immediate children? The phrase \"a node with retained children\" likely means any descendant that is retained, because otherwise you could remove a node while its grandchildren are retained, which would orphan them. The earlier description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we interpret as: if any node in its subtree (direct children, grandchildren, etc.) is retained, then this node cannot be removed. So we need to check entire subtree. Since we process deeper first, when we get to a node, any of its descendants that are retained are already decided, so we just check if any descendant is still in retained_ids. So we can implement a function has_retained_descendant(node_id) using parent pointers and DFS.\n- Also check that all required symbols are still present after removal. Compute current symbols count? We can maintain a set of symbols of retained nodes. For each required symbol, if the node we are removing is the only node with that symbol among retained nodes (excluding itself), then removal is not allowed. So we need to check symbol counts.\n\nIf both conditions satisfied, then remove the node: remove from retained_ids, add to removed_ids (in order of removal). Else, keep it retained.\n\nAfter processing all nodes, we have removed_node_ids (ordered by removal) and retained_node_ids. The output requires retained_node_ids sorted? The output schema says retained_node_ids: array of strings. The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we should sort retained_node_ids alphabetically or by any consistent order? The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs\". It likely means sorted by node ID (lexicographic). The output schema doesn't specify order, but we'll sort them. Also removal order should be the order in which nodes were removed (i.e., the order we added to removed_ids).\n\nImplement solve(case) that takes case dict and returns dict with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nWe also need to handle invalid cases: e.g., missing fields, cycles? Probably not needed. The spec says raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it. We could check that required fields exist: nodes, required_symbols. Also that each node has required fields. Also check that root exists and parent structure is tree. But for simplicity, we can assume valid input per fixture.\n\nNow write the code. Use standard library.\n\nWe'll also need to sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending. Since ids are strings, tie-break is string comparison. Use sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id'])).\n\nBuild parent map: id -> parent id (or None for root). Build child list for descendant check? For descendant check, we can do DFS from node: collect all descendants recursively using parent map (or build adjacency). Since depth is given, we could also mark nodes with a set of ancestors? Simpler: for each node, we can store list of children. Build children mapping. Then for a given node, we can search its subtree for any node still in retained_ids. Because subtree size is small (max 7 nodes in fixture), it's fine.\n\nWe also need map from symbol to list of node ids? For required symbol check, we need to know if there exists at least one retained node with that symbol after removal. We can precompute a count of retained nodes per symbol, initially count all nodes. Then when we tentatively remove, decrement count for the node's symbol and check if any required symbol count becomes 0. If yes, revert. This is efficient.\n\nBut careful: required symbols are symbols that must be represented. If a required symbol appears in multiple nodes, removing one is fine as long as at least one remains. So we need to maintain a dict symbol -> count of retained nodes. Initially set counts from all nodes. Then when we consider removal, we check if for all required symbols, count(symbol) > 1 or (count(symbol)==1 and node.symbol != symbol) to allow removal. Actually if count(symbol)==1 and the candidate node's symbol is that symbol, then after removal count becomes 0, so not allowed. So we check: for each req_sym, if count[req_sym] == 1 and node['symbol'] == req_sym: not allowed. If count[req_sym] == 0 already should not happen initially.\n\nFor descendant check: we need to ensure that no retained node is a descendant of this node. Since we process deeper first, we can precompute a set of ancestor IDs for each node (all ancestors up to root). For descendant check, we can check if any retained node has this node in its ancestor set. That might be easier: for each retained node (excluding candidate), get its ancestors; if candidate in ancestors, then candidate has retained descendant. But that is O(n^2) with n small. Alternatively, we can build a children adjacency and do a DFS from candidate among retained nodes. Since we are already iterating, we can just check all retained nodes: for each other retained node, see if its ancestor chain includes candidate. We can precompute ancestor sets.\n\nImplement: ancestor_of = { id: set of ancestor ids (including up to root) }. Compute by BFS/DFS from root. For each node, follow parent chain up to root, store. Then to check if candidate is ancestor of retained node: candidate in ancestor_of[descendant_id].\n\nWe also need to ensure we don't check candidate itself.\n\nNow algorithm steps:\n\n1. Validate input: case must have 'nodes' and 'required_symbols'. Raise ValueError if not.\n2. Build data structures:\n   - Map id -> node dict\n   - Map id -> children list\n   - Map id -> ancestor set (set of ancestor ids, not including self)\n   - Map symbol -> count of nodes (initial all nodes)\n   - Set of retained_ids = set(all node ids)\n   - List removed_ids = []\n3. Determine required_symbols set for fast lookup.\n4. Sort nodes by depth descending, id ascending.\n5. For each node in sorted_nodes:\n   - If not node['removable']: continue\n   - If node['symbol'] in required_symbols_set: continue (cannot remove required symbol)\n   - Check descendant condition: for any retained_id in retained_ids (excluding node), if node['id'] in ancestor_of[retained_id], then node has retained descendant -> cannot remove, continue.\n   - Check required symbols condition: compute if after removal all required symbols remain. For each req_sym in required_symbols_set:\n        if count[req_sym] == 1 and node['symbol'] == req_sym: not allowed, break.\n   - If both checks pass, then remove:\n        retained_ids.remove(node['id'])\n        removed_ids.append(node['id'])\n        count[node['symbol']] -= 1\n   - Else: node remains retained (already in retained_ids).\n6. After loop, retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained_ids), reverse=False) (id string sort)\n7. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}\n\nEdge cases: root is non-removable so will never be removed. Required symbols may be root's symbol? root symbol is 'root' and required includes 'root'. That's fine.\n\nNow write the code. Ensure JSON serializable output.\n\nThe implementation must be a string containing Python source. In the response, we write a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value that Python source string.\n\nWe need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nLet's write the solve function with proper indentation.\n\nWe'll include error handling: if case doesn't have required fields, raise ValueError.\n\nWe'll also include check for root existence: find node with parent None. Ensure exactly one? Not required.\n\nNow produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":3474,"total_tokens":5643,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2767},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}